WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.467
The Base forty four rehearsal also starts from the same definition.

00:00:04.467 --> 00:00:08.529
The generated app on screen is a real browser capture.

00:00:08.529 --> 00:00:16.245
It has three synthetic tickets, a searchable inbox, priority controls, an editable
detail view and a New ticket dialog.

00:00:16.245 --> 00:00:19.494
Its Ask AI panel explicitly says not connected.

00:00:19.494 --> 00:00:23.961
The capability panel says no database, no model and no connectors.

00:00:23.961 --> 00:00:27.616
Those are useful product statements, not defects to conceal.

00:00:27.616 --> 00:00:32.490
We have a visible baseline we can test before adding a backend.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:36.957
The layout differs from Lovable, but the user journey is recognizable.

00:00:36.957 --> 00:00:44.674
That is what a portable app definition should produce: the same small useful outcome
without requiring identical component markup.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.236
Open New ticket.

00:00:46.236 --> 00:00:54.884
Enter Workshop checkout check, choose the checkout training service and medium
priority, and use the invented description shown in the guide.

00:00:54.884 --> 00:01:03.121
The dialog explains that the record is added to this session only and that nothing
is saved to a server.

00:01:03.121 --> 00:01:08.475
After submission, the inbox count increases to four and the new ticket appears.

00:01:08.475 --> 00:01:11.770
The generated identifier differs from the Lovable identifier.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:15.064
That is expected because these are separate implementations.

00:01:15.064 --> 00:01:19.183
We compare the product behavior, not an accidental identifier format.

00:01:19.183 --> 00:01:24.537
Search for the title, filter by priority, clear the controls and inspect selection.

00:01:24.537 --> 00:01:31.950
Keep the synthetic-data and session-memory labels visible so nobody mistakes the
screen for a connected customer operations system.

00:01:32.292 --> 00:01:38.512
An editable field can change the current browser state without proving that anything
was stored durably.

00:01:38.512 --> 00:01:42.789
The Base forty four frontend supports in-memory edits in this rehearsal.

00:01:42.789 --> 00:01:44.344
A reload clears them.

00:01:44.344 --> 00:01:50.953
A database, record ownership, migration, backup and retention would be new
capabilities, each with its own consequences.

00:01:50.953 --> 00:01:55.619
Do not activate them just because the interface offers a persistence suggestion.

00:01:55.619 --> 00:02:03.783
Decide whether they are needed for the lesson, review the supported backend and
access rules, and preserve the simple baseline first.

00:02:03.783 --> 00:02:11.170
The first hour succeeds with a visible working app and one honest AI journey on the
selected supported route.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:17.390
The later course parts can add functionality to that same app without pretending it
already existed.

00:02:17.708 --> 00:02:21.818
The Ask AI panel states that there is no model connection.

00:02:21.818 --> 00:02:27.796
Clicking Analyze safely on this frontend must not invent a summary or imply a
provider call.

00:02:27.796 --> 00:02:31.532
An honest not-connected result is better than a polished fake.

00:02:31.532 --> 00:02:36.762
We can still test loading and failure states and explain the intended backend
contract.

00:02:36.762 --> 00:02:41.619
The model should receive only the validated synthetic task through a server-side
path.

00:02:41.619 --> 00:02:47.223
The browser must not hold a provider key, a Langfuse secret or a service credential.

00:02:47.223 --> 00:02:58.805
The fact that the platform includes some AI features does not prove that the current
plan exposes the server functions, secret storage and tool-capable model integration
needed by this specific harness.

00:02:58.805 --> 00:03:03.289
Discover the actual capability instead of inferring it from a product name.

00:03:03.625 --> 00:03:29.458
Your turn: follow the manual task. Pause for more time.

00:03:29.458 --> 00:03:38.919
In the account and plan used for this comparison, the required server-function,
protected-secret and native tool-model route is not available as an accepted
implementation.

00:03:38.919 --> 00:03:43.649
This recording therefore shows a real frontend workflow and a porting explanation.

00:03:43.649 --> 00:03:49.956
It does not claim a fully authenticated Base forty four agent or a matching Langfuse
trace.

00:03:49.956 --> 00:03:57.840
We do not upgrade the plan, buy credits, invent a server, or weaken security to make
the comparison appear complete.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.599
A concrete limit helps the learner decide.

00:04:00.599 --> 00:04:12.425
They can use a supported plan and backend after review, export the frontend and
implement an authorized adapter, or continue with the accepted Lovable route while
studying the same concepts.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:16.418
A backend adapter is more than a fetch call.

00:04:16.418 --> 00:04:26.201
It must establish app identity, validate input, enforce the selected service and
small tool registry, reserve quota safely, cap execution and return structured
states.

00:04:26.201 --> 00:04:29.869
The explicit change stop should run before model construction.

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:35.168
Model and tool failures should be observable without leaking secrets or user
payloads.

00:04:35.168 --> 00:04:40.059
The app should show actual returned model and usage only when reported.

00:04:40.059 --> 00:04:46.173
If the platform's native integration cannot provide those controls, do not describe
it as equivalent.

00:04:46.173 --> 00:04:53.103
Document what it can do, what wrapper is required, and which tests will demonstrate
the missing boundary.

00:04:53.103 --> 00:05:00.032
The same reasoning applies to any visual agent platform or hosted workflow service,
not just this builder.

00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:11.916
When the Base forty four backend is properly implemented and its telemetry
destination is approved, give it a separate named Langfuse project, such as OpsDesk
Base forty four.

00:05:11.916 --> 00:05:15.640
Keep the ingestion key in that backend's protected configuration.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:20.604
Do not reuse a key from another app simply because it works.

00:05:20.604 --> 00:05:27.636
Separate project bindings make it easier to attribute runs, apply retention and
access decisions, and compare implementations.

00:05:27.636 --> 00:05:33.428
Then test one bounded synthetic operation and independently match its trace and
random session.

00:05:33.428 --> 00:05:40.461
Until that happens, a code snippet, successful settings form or visible trace panel
is not provider acceptance.

00:05:40.461 --> 00:05:49.148
Keep the current status explicit in the video, transcript and course rather than
letting one green example stand for all builders.

00:05:49.458 --> 00:05:55.481
Pause and recreate the visible Base forty four journey if you want to compare it
yourself.

00:05:55.481 --> 00:05:56.987
Use invented tickets only.

00:05:56.987 --> 00:06:06.775
Check that the created ticket can be found and selected, that the priority controls
behave correctly, and that the AI panel is honest about its connection.

00:06:06.775 --> 00:06:09.034
Then make a small capability list.

00:06:09.034 --> 00:06:11.669
Can the supported backend keep secrets server-side?

00:06:11.669 --> 00:06:13.551
Can it require app authentication?

00:06:13.551 --> 00:06:17.692
Can it execute a schema-validated tool and enforce a bounded loop?

00:06:17.692 --> 00:06:19.574
Can it export safe telemetry?

00:06:19.574 --> 00:06:23.715
Do not answer those questions from a marketing feature list alone.

00:06:23.715 --> 00:06:25.974
Inspect the actual source and runtime.

00:06:25.974 --> 00:06:33.879
Choose the route whose limits you can explain and whose essential behavior you can
verify with the resources you already own.

00:06:34.208 --> 00:07:00.000
Your turn: follow the manual task. Pause for more time.
